17592186045417] [17592186483382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483382 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483382 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483382 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3t-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "858d57fe-416e-56c5-af8b-4ea6e4298062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483382 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =4t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =t\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}36(t-1)-(3t+1) \\\\ -(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}33t-37 \\\\ -3t-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into {{field-3}} ." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc370c50-c95d-53af-9285-c137ac273145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483418 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Let's substitute ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$ from $\\text{L4}$ into $\\text{L3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+\\cancel{AΦu(t)}=\\cancel{AΦu(t)}+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3}\\text{ and }\\text{L4}$ by substitution.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into  $\\text{L1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186497428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186497449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186497452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186497458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186497486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496656 #edb/ref 10